    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 23rd with a clear offensive engine, led by **Yordan Alvarez** (80.32) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (78.10), who serve as our highest-upside anchors. **Yordan Alvarez** continues to operate in an elite tier thanks to a .419 xwOBA, while **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against Teng and a confirmed spot at the top of the order, providing the speed floor we prioritize to chase stolen base production. **Christian Yelich** also earns a strong recommendation (74.33) as he looks to exploit the defensive weaknesses of Roki Sasaki, bolstered by a significant +13% platoon edge. While we are still awaiting official confirmation for several spots, the optimizer is leaning heavily into **Colt Keith** (69.64) and **Trea Turner** (69.74), the latter of whom remains a premier play due to his ability to exploit Slade Cecconi\u2019s slow delivery, offsetting a slight penalty for wind blowing in.\n\nWe are navigating a few tactical hurdles, specifically regarding our catchers and infield utility. **Dillon Dingler** (60.87) edges out the rest of the fringe starters due to a +16% park factor and the high-leverage #2 spot in the lineup, despite a drag from a dynamic platoon disadvantage. The most difficult decision rested with our lower-tier starters; **Hyeseong Kim** (50.83) barely maintains his spot over bench options due to the scarcity of high-speed profiles, even though his matchup against Robert Gasser carries a notable risk of regression. **Junior Caminero** was our primary consideration for a spot start, but he is sidelined today by a rainout, removing any possibility of his involvement. As we monitor the pending lineups, we must remain vigilant regarding **Gary Sanchez** and **Luke Raley**, both of whom are projected as marginal starts; should any late-inning weather alerts arise or lineups shift, we are prepared to pivot to more favorable matchups to preserve our limited game caps for the remainder of the week.",
